     1	# Step 9 package contract
     2	
     3	`handoff.json` is the authored source of truth. `HANDOFF.md` is its readable checklist, generated by the checker. Keep one set of criterion IDs; do not maintain a second design/QA acceptance list. Freeze the source JSON, reference frames and all linked evidence by version/hash. Any affected change requires fresh checks and approval. Paths in the JSON are resolved from the command's working directory; use absolute paths for release evidence to avoid ambiguity.
     4	
     5	This contract specifies what each real project must supply. The included synthetic fixture is only an executable checker example. It is not a real project's copy, factual proof, approval, licensed asset set or completed handoff.
     6	
     7	## Fixed package contents
     8	
     9	| Artifact | Required contents and exact acceptance |
    10	|---|---|
    11	| `handoff.json` and generated `HANDOFF.md` | Version 1 record; project/phase; no open questions; no placeholders; all screens, locales and states; one criterion ID per check; actual evidence and owner/reviewer |
    12	| Source snapshot | Project settings, rules, brief, fact register and copy-deck revisions; source hashes and cited lines; current step and permitted scope |
    13	| Private design link | `designLink.url`, `private:true` and evidence of access/privacy; tool and frame IDs/version; no public upload as a shortcut |
    14	| `DESIGN.md` | Reviewed narrative and token frontmatter; Google format lint with zero errors; token decisions traced to brief lines |
    15	| Tokens | DTCG-style JSON containing type, spacing, colors and breakpoints; generated CSS variables; executable build script, actual config and locked dependency versions; no hand-edited generated CSS |
    16	| Screen/state inventory | Stable screen and component IDs; default, hover, focus, disabled, empty, loading and error all accounted for, plus every additional reachable state and form behavior below; N/A requires reason, reviewer and evidence; applicable states specify appearance, behavior and recovery |
    17	| Reference frames | 1440-pixel-wide PNGs; `screen--state--locale--vNN.png`; actual file hash and version; every applicable state/language; reference implementation HTML when used |
    18	| Final copy deck | Per-slot ID, screen, purpose, kind, exact final text, min/max character range, tone, language/direction, source lines, claim IDs and placement screenshot; variants use actual approved translations |
    19	| Assets | File, source URL/document, creator, licence/permission, consent record where needed, intended use and crop; font family/weight files and licence; file size and project's budget result |
    20	| Motion | Job, duration in ms, easing, trigger, affected element, interruption/cleanup, static reduced-motion fallback, no-JS and script-failure behavior |
    21	| Acceptance | Per-screen criteria with ID, BLOCK/WARN, observable pass/fail check, decision and evidence. Failed BLOCK stops. WARN needs impact, owner and approved disposition with no unresolved question |
    22	| Accessibility | WCAG 2.2 AA, contrast-pair table, focus order and captures, target sizes/exceptions, alt text and intent, heading outline, standalone action labels, manual audit and automated incomplete-result resolution |
    23	| Audit intent | One H1 per page; complete semantic outline; intent for every image's alt text; independent link/button labels; page purpose, search intent, proposed title/description, index/canonical decision, schema type and source-backed properties or an explicit reason for none |
    24	| Approval and critic | Final scoped approval with date, approver and every frame ID/hash; the approver's feedback log with every item resolved or explicitly deferred (plan, "Folding in the approver's feedback"), recorded as the `feedback-log` manual check; independent critic rounds, reference work, row scores and PASS/FAIL; no open P0/P1 |
    25	| Tool evidence | Runtime/tool versions, exact commands, stdout/exit, machine JSON and screenshots; declared test-only evidence excluded from actual approval |
    26	
    27	Tokens include the desktop reference width and documented layout breakpoints needed for the authorized scope. They do not invent mobile/tablet styling while those phases are deferred. Breakpoint CSS variables are a handoff representation; standard CSS custom properties cannot be substituted directly into media-query conditions. The engineer translates those values into the project's existing build conventions.
    28	
    29	## JSON fields
    30	
    31	`tools/handoff-contract.mjs` is the executable validator. Use its field names exactly; do not infer an unvalidated schema from prose. The following is its authoring guide:
    32	
    33	- `version`, `phase`, `brief.path`, `rules.path`, and `settings` identify the contract and inputs. Settings provides `path`, `repository`, `stack` and `roles.engineer`, `roles.qaGate`, `roles.writer`, `roles.approver`, matching the actual project settings. Fannan copies those values; it never selects a stack. `settings.desktopWidths` lists the project's declared desktop CSS widths: unique positive integers that always include the canonical 1440 (for example `[1440]` or `[1440, 1280]`); a missing, duplicate or 1440-less list fails the contract in both phases (the wireframe is a desktop handoff too; there is no default list). It declares widths to check; it does not authorize a mobile phase.
    34	- `pointOfView.idea` and `pointOfView.briefRefs` establish the visual thesis. A separate decision/section ledger links every section, grid break, token and expressive element to source lines and the relevant audience task.
    35	- `languages` lists each `{lang,dir}`. Every screen has its page, `headingOutline`, `seoIntent`, `schemaIntent`, `images` with alt text/intent, and `actions` with standalone labels. Empty image/action arrays must match the real page; omission is not evidence of absence.
    36	- `screens[].states` accounts for the seven state names **per language**, with `lang` and `dir`. Applicable states have `page` and `frame:{path,width:1440,version,sha256}`. A non-applicable state has `applicable:false`, `reason`, `reviewer` and `evidence`; default is always applicable. A page-level state may show the relevant component in that state; do not invent a global error screen for a static page.
    37	- Shared component inventory (`components`, `component-consistency`): Maintain one canonical specification for each reused component: anatomy, semantic variants, sizes, token bindings, content constraints and composition/usage rules. Map every screen instance to that specification and the existing component where available. Compare equivalent instances across screens; unexplained differences fail. Each entry is `{id, variants, states, tokens, behaviour}` (optional `anatomy`, `sizes`, `usage`, `content`); ids are unique, `states` includes `default`, and `tokens` maps each property to a token (it may be empty in the wireframe phase, never at handoff). Every `screens[].components[]` entry names a shared `id`, may choose a declared `variant`, and accounts for its states, whose applicable names must be in the shared definition; a specification field it repeats must equal the shared definition, so one component specified differently on two screens fails. The critic's final round records `componentComparison: {screens, decision, evidence}` covering every screen.
    38	- `screens[].components[].states` also accounts for each state, including justified N/A. Attach appearance, behavior, entry/exit triggers, keyboard behavior and recovery in the component specification referenced by the inventory. Every non-default component state must be represented by a real reference frame or state sheet.
    39	- Reachable states beyond the seven (component specification and `interaction-states`): The seven baseline states are a minimum. Derive additional reachable states from actual interactions, including success/completion, selected/checked, expanded/collapsed, validation and read-only where applicable. Specify triggers, appearance, approved copy, semantics, announcements, keyboard/focus behavior and reference evidence. Success communicates the confirmed outcome and next action. The interaction critique fails when a reachable state or transition is unspecified.
    40	- Forms and consequential actions (component specification and `form-behavior`): For every form, specify labels, required/optional indicators, accepted formats, help, validation timing, inline/summary errors, failure focus, input preservation and correction steps. Define submitting, success, failure, retry and duplicate-submission behavior. For consequential actions, specify cancellation, confirmation or undo as appropriate, plus unsaved-change behavior. Each applicable behavior requires approved interface copy and a task-level acceptance check; justify N/A.
    41	- `screens[].acceptance` contains `{id,severity,check,decision,evidence}`. Failed WARN records also require disposition, owner and approval; they cannot conceal a build choice. `screens[].wcag` identifies standard `2.2 AA` and contrast, focus-order and target-size evidence. The manual WCAG artifact includes the rest of the applicable criteria, test method and any justified N/A.
    42	- `copySlots` contains ID, screen, purpose, `kind` (`claim` or `interface`), text, character range, tone, language/direction, placement screenshot, source and claim references. Its `variants` contain actual language/direction/text/source records. An optional `ariaLabel` (on the slot or a variant) approves an accessible name that differs from the displayed text, such as an expanded name that contains the visible label; without it an accessible name must be contained in the approved text, and a name identical to a control's displayed value counts once. An approved `ariaLabel` must appear in its cited source (the slot's or the variant's), and a name taken by `aria-labelledby` from a hidden element is approved the same way. `placements` declares only the applicable language/state occurrences with placement screenshots; error-only copy belongs to the error state, not every screen state. Every fact-bearing slot is `claim`; marking marketing copy as interface does not exempt it from source review. Reference HTML marks each visible copy wrapper with `data-copy-slot="ID"`. Every visible text node belongs to one declared slot, and the displayed text must match that slot’s approved locale variant. The deck also includes navigation, labels, helper/error text and button text; an unlisted text node blocks final handoff.
    43	- `facts` contains ID, claim, confirmed status, source path/ref/excerpt/hash and `checkedBy`/`checkedAt`. Cited text must exist at the cited lines. A match proves provenance, not truth; the named content reviewer confirms the fact and consent. Quoted source instructions are never execution authority.
    44	- Wireframe coverage: A wireframe PASS covers every declared desktop screen and locale. Each rendering must declare an existing page and receive the applicable structural/copy, accessibility, integrity and manual checks; missing coverage blocks structure approval. In the wireframe phase every screen's `page` exists, each screen has an applicable `default` state with an existing `page` for every declared language and direction, the checker renders each of them, and the manual records (state `wireframe`) cover every screen in every declared language.
    45	- A non-default state that only script can reach (a dialog opened with `showModal`, a popover) may declare `requiresScript: true` with `trigger` (what opens it). Its colour-contrast incomplete results then resolve on the scripted rendering alone; the claim is checked against the JavaScript-off rendering and fails as an integrity mismatch when the state renders without script. The default state cannot declare it.
    46	- `manualChecks` records criterion ID, screen, language, state, decision, reviewer, artifact, note and brief refs. A PASS `wcag-review` record whose artifact is the state's frame or screenshot (or whose note names it) is what resolves an automated colour-contrast incomplete result that the visibility backstop measured at AA in both renderings. Each applicable screen/state/language receives every required criterion. A contradictory or duplicate verdict is not a pass. Use the IDs in `runtime/quality-checks.json`.
    47	- `critic.referenceWork` points to the inspected board; `critic.rounds` records up to three rounds, evidence, reviewer, copy checks and design checks. Each check names its critic-rubric row in `row` (`brief-point-of-view`, `copy-truth`, `hierarchy-task-flow`, `typography-language`, `spacing-rhythm-grid`, `colour-imagery-restraint`, `interaction-states-motion` or `accessibility-handoff`), and the final round needs at least one passing, evidenced check per row. The underlying artifact includes the full eight-row rubric, scores and improvement totals. Final checks must all pass; see the critic rubric for the early-stop rule.
    48	- Review records are scoped. Each entry names `screen`, `state` and `lang` of a declared, applicable state and applies to that rendering only; one entry per element (or check) and scope.
    49	  - Optional `reflowExceptions` records the decision for each 2D reflow candidate the 320 px screen reports: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `id` is the candidate element's id and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. An unresolved candidate blocks the final handoff, as does an id that names different elements in one scope.
    50	  - Optional `spacingExceptions` backs each `data-fannan-spacing-exception` on the page: `{id, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `reviewer` is the approver. An exception without a matching entry blocks the final handoff.
    51	  - Optional `jsOffDifferences` records an expected JavaScript-off difference: `{check, screen, state, lang, reason, reviewer}`, where `check` is one integrity field (`glass`, `h1`, `placeholders`, `centered`, `typeSizes`, `heroGradient`, `purpleBlue`, `iconRows`, `accent`, `accentRatio`, `maxViewportAccent`, `filler`, `ruleOfThree`, `frames`, `stylesheetsUnscanned`, `spacingOffScale`, `horizontalOverflow`, `clipped`, `offscreenLeft`, `unreachable`, `scrolledText`, `unmeasured`, `unscannedScopes`, `reflowFail`, `contrastFailures`, `contrastUnknown`, `contrastChecked`, `contrastEffects`, `textMeasured`, `textObscured`, `textUnreadable`, `textUnmeasured`, `textHidden` or `content`) and `reviewer` is the qaGate or approver. A reason without a reviewer excuses nothing; without a matching entry the difference fails as an integrity mismatch. Different JavaScript-off content (copy slots and headings compared with their text) is never PASS: unexplained it is a mismatch, and with a reviewed `content` entry the entry is recorded but the cross-check stays INCOMPLETE, because the JavaScript-off metrics then prove nothing (R13 N14). Zero JavaScript-off contrast coverage, where the scripted page had coverage, is INCOMPLETE too. The rendered-visibility backstop (`references/design-quality.md`) adds `textMeasured`, `textObscured`, `textUnreadable`, `textUnmeasured` and `textHidden` per rendering: text obscured or unreadable with JavaScript on fails, text lost with JavaScript off (including more text clipped to nothing) is a mismatch that a reviewed entry records but never makes PASS, and unmeasurable text is INCOMPLETE. Text clipped to nothing by an ancestor that cannot be scrolled, the same in both renderings, is not displayed (`textHidden`) and is graded through the declared states. Pixels decide visibility; AA stays with the CSS pass whenever it resolves the colours. Unresolved compositing on displayed text in either rendering blocks handoff as INCOMPLETE. Token contrast proofs and `jsOffDifferences` cannot waive it. Retaining the effect requires a reviewed measurement implementation that resolves its actual composition for every applicable rendering. Backdrop-filter remains a hard FAIL. The cross-check runs on the positional page and on every declared state; the main gates still fail scripted glass and the other hard fails.
    52	- `designLink`, `tokens`, `assets`, `motion`, `toolEvidence`, `approval`, `openQuestions` and `placeholders` complete the package. Each of `tokens.json`, `tokens.css`, `tokens.buildScript`, `tokens.config` and `tokens.lock` has `{path,sha256}`. `tokens.buildEvidence.path` points to the `.build.json` receipt written by the reviewed Style Dictionary build, binding input, output, script, config and lock hashes. The checker never executes an arbitrary project-supplied script. Approval names its record, decision, approver and `frameIds` in the form `screen/lang/state/version/sha256`; the dated approval artifact repeats those exact IDs.
    53	
    54	Character counts are not permission to shorten approved content. For a multilingual slot use language-specific ranges when necessary, revise the contract and revalidate. Rendering, grapheme clusters and bidirectional order require visual review even if a string-length check passes.
    55	
    56	## Evidence and responsibilities
    57	
    58	The writer owns exact copy, fact confirmation and wording review. Fannan owns design decisions and the package. The critic reviews independently. The approver owns the human decision. The project's engineer confirms that the package leaves no build question and implements only in the repository/stack from settings. The QA gate reuses the same criterion IDs against the implementation and records preview URL, commit SHA, build output, screenshots, test steps and actual result. Fannan's prototype evidence cannot stand in for that implementation QA.
    59	
    60	The generated checklist is a review surface, not an authorization token. Compare its source JSON hash and artifact versions before relying on it. A full PASS requires all checks from the current machine gate plus verified manual artifacts and actual approval. A manually typed `PASS`, a copied report or a valid hash of fabricated evidence is insufficient; inspect provenance and authorship during review.
    61	
    62	## Missing inputs and change control
    63	
    64	At draft phases, keep missing text visibly marked and register its owner. At Step 9, even one unresolved question or placeholder blocks. Missing licences, references, claims, tool evidence, role mappings or approval are treated the same way. The issue list states the exact item, owner, next action and impact. The task remains Stuck/On hold; there is no partial handoff labelled complete.
    65	
    66	After final approval, a change to source copy, tokens, state behavior, frame or acceptance requirement invalidates affected checks and approval. Create a fresh version and output directory; never overwrite evidence already cited by approval. Reuse unaffected evidence only when its dependency hashes and scope still match.
# Mi'mar R19b note: script-only declared states (Mi'mar's tiebreak)

Branch `mimar-r4-fixes`, from 3dabdf9. Last code/package commit: 301ac59. Written Oct 18 2026, about 12:28 PM Gaza (UTC+3). This note follows the R19 record (`reviews/MIMAR-R19-changes-5890da8.md`, now hashed in the registry) and is the latest round record.

Registry: `trust/R3-CHANGES.sha256` sha256 `d9fd58347b9b5cde262abe7f7c82d7a3a451d4751192b66e131e9cdc87ea6730`. Fadel's floors and the threat model unchanged.

## Decision (Mi'mar, 12:01 PM)

A declared NON-default state that only script can reach (its trigger recorded, no JavaScript-off rendering of it) may resolve an axe colour-contrast incomplete on the scripted rendering alone, when that exact element measured at AA for its size, a PASS `wcag-review` record cites the screenshot, and the declaration has `requiresScript: true`. The default state still needs both renderings. A `requiresScript` claim on a state that renders with JavaScript off fails as a mismatch.

## Implementation

- Contract: `requiresScript` must be a boolean; `true` only on a non-default state with a non-empty `trigger`.
- `requiresScriptCheck` (visibility.mjs): the claim is valid when the state is not `default`, the trigger is recorded and the state does not render with JavaScript off. "Renders with JavaScript off" means every element the scripted rendering shows (measured text, by composed path) is also shown in the JavaScript-off rendering.
- A false claim adds a `requiresScript/js-off` integrity mismatch (state or positional page), so the state fails.
- `resolveContrastIncomplete(..., scriptOnly)` uses the scripted measurement for both conditions only when the claim is valid; otherwise unchanged.

## Results

- Chrome test (`R19b` in `tests/panel-r19.test.mjs`): scripted modal with the claim resolves; without the claim, without a trigger, on `default`, below AA (2.8:1) or without a review it stays blocking; a static state with the claim is a false claim.
- Full pre-handoff run on the scripted modal fixture: state PASS, axe incomplete resolved (`requiresScript.valid` true); the undeclared modal stays blocking; the false claim is `requiresScript/js-off`.
- Before/after (`test-results/r19/R19b-*`; before = 3dabdf9): Chrome 6 of 8 before (the R19b test and the contract test fail at their assertions), 8 of 8 after; docs 12 of 13 before, 13 of 13 after; fixture R19b expectations false before, exit 0 after. No load errors.
- Full suites: node 134/134; without deps 76 + 58 SKIPPED; Python 10; r2 4 + 4; package 41 / 36 + 5 SKIPPED; self-test PASS; syntax 0 failures; lint 0 errors; fixture exit 0; Node 20 128 PASS, 6 FAIL (the known Style Dictionary tests).

## Untested

The trigger is recorded, not executed. A script-only state whose JavaScript-off rendering shows the same elements with different text is refused as a false claim. See `release/UNTESTED.md` (R19b).
# R3 checks not established

## R19 additions (Mi'mar, 18 October 2026, panel round 19)

All browser checks below ran in Chrome 155 headless only, at 1440 × 900 and device scale one. Panel round 19 had one reviewer (GPT), cross-checked by Mi'mar. Scope follows the threat model in `FANNAN-PLAN.md`.

- **Width screen, hidden content (R19-GPT-01):** content a collapsed box (no inner width or height) hides, and content wholly beyond the inline-end or block-end edge of a box that cannot scroll, is not a clip; content pushed wholly past the start edge and displayed content cut partly still fail. Executed: max-height:0 accordion, LTR and RTL carousel tracks, a partly cut slide, text pushed past the start edge, the R12/R13 width suites. UNTESTED: vertical writing modes, carousels that place inactive slides before the active one (they fail as a start-edge clip), clip-path and contain:paint clippers.
- **Hidden layers (R19-GPT-02, R19-GPT-03 P3):** positioned `::before`/`::after` that paint (on the text's element or an ancestor) and positioned `pointer-events:none` layers that paint are possible covers, graded by pixels at 3:1; negative z-index layers are skipped; a gradient pseudo-element on a clipping ancestor is a truncation fade and the lines under it are not displayed; an SVG is a translucent cover. Executed: stuck and script-removed veils (both kinds), a 70% veil, a read-more fade, a decorative SVG wave, link underlines and accent bars as pseudo-elements, a z-index -1 scrim, a sticky table header, the R18 and R19 honest sets, the real built site. UNTESTED: pseudo-elements positioned with percentages, calc() or transforms (the box is taken from px offsets, else the owner's box), nested pseudo-element stacking, masks and clip-path on the layer, a pointer-events:none layer inside a closed shadow root, an opaque SVG drawn over text (now graded by pixels rather than obscured).
- **Inner scrollports (R19-GPT-04):** after each document pass the end of every visible scrollable element is visited and new content triggers another pass (bounded as before). UNTESTED: nested scrollports whose end only appears after the outer one scrolls, horizontal lazy lists in RTL, IntersectionObservers with rootMargin far outside the scrollport.
- **Colour-contrast incomplete resolver (R19-MIMAR-01; Fadel's tiebreak):** resolved only for an element the backstop measured, every line uncovered, at 4.5:1 (3:1 large) in both renderings, with a PASS `wcag-review` record citing the screenshot. The measure is the 90th-percentile ink ratio of each line (the lowest line counts), not a per-glyph minimum. UNTESTED: axe targets inside shadow roots or frames (not resolved, stay blocking), elements whose text is in several child elements (each needs its own measurement), and a declared state opened by script: its JavaScript-off rendering cannot show the dialog, so a colour-contrast incomplete on dialog text stays blocking (seen in the fixture run; a decision for Fadel). Dark text over a photo that CSS resolves against the page background is still passed by the backstop when its peak is at least 1.5:1 (axe's incomplete keeps it blocked).
- **Script-only states (R19b; Mi'mar's tiebreak):** a non-default state declared `requiresScript: true` with its `trigger` resolves a colour-contrast incomplete on the scripted rendering alone; the claim is false (integrity mismatch) when every element the scripted rendering shows is also shown with JavaScript off. UNTESTED: a script-only state whose JavaScript-off rendering shows the same elements with different text (the claim is then refused as false), and whether the recorded trigger really opens the state (it is recorded, not executed).
- **Top layer on declared states (R19-MIMAR-02; Fadel's tiebreak):** on a state other than `default`, page text outside an open `:modal` dialog (or a shown `aria-modal="true"` element) is counted as not displayed, page text an open popover covers is not graded, the dialog's and popover's own text are graded; a non-modal open dialog keeps covered text graded (obscured). UNTESTED: nested modals, `inert` set by script without a modal, fullscreen elements, customisable select pickers and other UA top-layer surfaces, aria-modal elements that do not cover the page.
- **Copy deck (R19-GPT-05/06 P3):** an `aria-labelledby` name with a hidden or undisplayed reference is an accessible name to approve; an approved `ariaLabel` must appear in its cited source. UNTESTED: `aria-labelledby` across shadow roots, `aria-description`, names from `<label>` elements that are hidden.

## R18 additions (Mi'mar, 18 October 2026, panel round 18)

All browser checks below ran in Chrome 155 headless only, at 1440 × 900 and device scale one. Panel round 18 had one reviewer (GPT), cross-checked by Mi'mar. Scope follows the threat model in `FANNAN-PLAN.md`.

- **Backstop role (R18-MIMAR-02, R18-GPT-04; Fadel's tiebreak):** pixels decide visibility, not AA. Where the CSS pass resolves the colours, pixels fail a line only when its strongest ink pixel is under 1.5:1 or it has fewer than three ink pixels; where it does not (background images, filters, blends, translucent covers), the 90th-percentile ink must reach 3:1. Only opaque covers (replaced elements, background colours at 90% or more opacity) obscure. Executed: Lato, Inter and Inter Tight at weights 100-400, 12-48 px, in #1a1a1a, #595959 and #767676 (180 combinations, 0 failures), a 3% white layer, the honest-pattern set below. UNTESTED: other fonts, hinting and device scale 2; text over photographs where the CSS pass is unknown and the photo is pale (graded at 3:1 by pixels, so a thin weight there can still fail); a cover between 3% and 90% opacity is graded by pixels at 3:1; only the 3% layer and the gradient fade were executed.
- **Lines from text ranges (R18-GPT-03/07):** each text node's word ranges are grouped into line boxes; the middle 60% of each line box is sampled. UNTESTED: vertical writing modes, ruby, text with negative letter spacing or overlapping lines (line-height under 1), pseudo-element text, SVG text, text nodes longer than 4,000 words (the rest is not grouped).
- **Clipped text (R18-MIMAR-01, R18-GPT-05):** text clipped to nothing by an ancestor that cannot be scrolled is not displayed (`textHidden`); text outside a scrollable ancestor's scrollport is scrolled into view (vertical and horizontal), and every scrolled ancestor is restored afterwards. Executed: carousel with and without autoplay, max-height accordion, script-opened accordion (JavaScript-off mismatch), app shell, horizontal tab strip. UNTESTED: `overflow: hidden` panels that script scrolls (treated as not scrollable, so their other text is hidden); `contain: paint` and `clip-path` on an ancestor as clippers (not used for hiding decisions; clip-path is caught by pixels); scroll snapping and scroll-driven animations that react to inner scrolling; nested scrollers more than two deep; text hidden this way in every rendering and in every declared state is never graded (that is the intended scope: declared states grade it).
- **Coverage (R18-GPT-06):** target-directed scan, at most 300 steps and four passes; early stop or growth after the last pass is INCOMPLETE. Executed: a 40,000 px page, an IntersectionObserver footer, an endless list (INCOMPLETE at a 25-step bound), the real built site (12 and 8 steps). UNTESTED: virtualised lists that remove earlier rows (targets no longer in the document are not graded), pages where every step adds content (they stop at the bound; estimated under a minute per rendering from the 25-step run), content that appears only after a timer with page scripts on and no scrolling.
- **Copy deck (R18-GPT-01, R18-GPT-02 P3):** a slot's optional `ariaLabel` (or a variant's) approves the accessible name separately; a name identical to the displayed value counts once; option labels are screened. UNTESTED: `aria-labelledby` names (not matched to the deck), `aria-description`, accessible names on display:contents elements.

## R17 additions (Mi'mar, 18 October 2026, panel round 17)

All browser checks below ran in Chrome 155 headless only, at 1440 × 900 and device scale one. Panel round 17 had one reviewer (GPT), cross-checked by Mi'mar. Scope follows the threat model in `FANNAN-PLAN.md`.

- **Rendered-visibility backstop (R17-MIMAR-01/02, R17-GPT-02):** hit test plus screenshot-pixel contrast in both renderings (method in `references/design-quality.md`, code in `tools/visibility.mjs`). Executed: clip-path, `scale(.02)`, a white full-viewport preloader, `::placeholder` opacity, `-webkit-text-fill-color` and a gradient overlay, each removed by script, are a JavaScript-off mismatch in Chrome and in full pre-handoff runs; a permanent fade overlay, a permanent faint fill and text clipped to nothing fail or are INCOMPLETE; a long healthy page (sticky header, grey text on light bands, icon button, controls, visually hidden label, dark footer) and normal animations (load fade-in, IntersectionObserver scroll reveal with a `noscript` fallback, an infinite spinner, a requestAnimationFrame tween) pass with JavaScript on and off, as do a skip link above or left of the page, a closed `<details>` menu, a `display:none` menu, a disabled button, a thin grey separator glyph and content that grows the page. Healthy-page survey outside the fixture (box only, external requests blocked): a built bilingual company site (English and Arabic, GSAP scroll reveals, served locally) showed false positives with the first version (closed menu, skip link, script tweens, a growing page); after the fixes it has none, with JavaScript on or off. Twenty draft section pages showed only text the CSS pass also fails (light grey labels, faded placeholders) after a thin-glyph and disabled-control fix. UNTESTED: text over photographs, video or canvas (the modal background can be a photo colour); very thin or tiny fonts near the 3:1 floor (antialiasing lowers the measured ink); pseudo-element and SVG text (not targets); text inside closed shadow roots, frames, `<details>` and open select lists; horizontally scrolled containers (text outside the scrollport is clipped to nothing, INCOMPLETE); continuous script motion (an autoplaying carousel) ends each band's settle at the 2 s bound and is measured mid-motion (counted in `unsettledBands`); scroll-linked (scrubbed) reveals are measured at the band's scroll position; pages taller than about 20 viewports (run time grows by one screenshot per half viewport); other viewports and device scales. A covering element with `pointer-events: none` is not in the hit-test stack; only the pixels catch it.
- **Hiding by opacity alone:** text left rendered with `opacity: 0` (a closed menu, an inactive tab) is unreadable to the backstop and fails with JavaScript on. Use `hidden`, `display: none` or `visibility: hidden` for inactive content.
- **Controls (R17-GPT-01):** a control's displayed value (button-type input value, or the browser's default label for submit and reset without one) is compared separately from its accessible name. UNTESTED: file-selector and date-picker labels, `<button>` elements whose label is CSS generated content.
- **Native copy (R17-GPT-03):** button-type input values, placeholders, every option label of a shown select, datalist options, `aria-label`, image `alt` and `title` are screened for placeholders and filler; values, placeholders, option labels and `aria-label` must sit in a copy slot whose deck text contains them. Typed or fixture values of text fields are user data and are not matched. UNTESTED: `aria-description`, `aria-placeholder`, live-region text and text that script writes after load into native attributes only.
- **Filter decision:** unchanged and fail-closed: unresolved compositing on text is INCOMPLETE in either rendering; the pixel-measured exception is ENH backlog, not built.

## R16 additions (Mi'mar, 18 October 2026, panel round 16)

All browser checks below ran in Chrome 155 headless only. Panel round 16 had one reviewer (GPT), cross-checked by Mi'mar. Scope follows the threat model in `FANNAN-PLAN.md`.

- **Compositing (R16-GPT-01):** `filter: opacity()` is composited like opacity; identity filters are ignored; any other filter function, a blend mode, a backdrop filter or a mask anywhere on the text's composed ancestor chain makes that text unknown (`contrastEffects`), and the cross-check is INCOMPLETE in either rendering. Consequence: a page that keeps such an effect on text cannot pass the integrity cross-check, by design. UNTESTED: effects on pseudo-elements or SVG text; `filter` values set through `url()` to an SVG filter that is in fact an identity (counted unknown); text painted inside a canvas.
- **Native controls (R16-GPT-02):** values, placeholders (with the `::placeholder` colour), button-type input labels and a select's chosen option are contrast-checked; controls, links, images and elements with a role or ARIA label are compared with their accessible name and displayed value. The accessible name is an approximation of the specification (aria-labelledby, aria-label, label elements, value, alt, own text, title, placeholder), not the full algorithm. UNTESTED: `::file-selector-button`, date and time pickers, autofill colours, `<option>` lists while open, names from CSS generated content. As before, an unexplained JavaScript-off content difference records a content mismatch and the cross-check status is INCOMPLETE (R13 N14).
- **Wireframe coverage (R16-GPT-03):** the wireframe phase requires each screen's page to exist, an applicable default rendering per declared language, manual records per screen and language, and renders each declared screen/language with phase-appropriate checks (no frame hash, type, accent, spacing or placeholder thresholds yet). Links inside the pages (navigation between screens) are not followed.
- **Component inventory (R16-GPT-04):** the contract enforces one shared definition per component, references by id, declared variants and states, and equality of any repeated specification field; the critic records a component comparison covering every screen. Whether two instances that do not repeat a field are visually consistent is a reviewer judgment (`component-consistency`), not machine-checked.

## R15 additions (Mi'mar, 18 October 2026, panel round 15)

All browser checks below ran in Chrome 155 headless only. Panel round 15 had one reviewer (GPT); B4 was found by Mi'mar alone. Scope follows the threat model in `FANNAN-PLAN.md`.

- **Width screen (A1):** an `overflow-y: auto`/`scroll` ancestor holds lower text only when its scrollport ends inside the visible viewport (and above every clipping ancestor) and its scroll range reaches the text. UNTESTED: scrollers moved by transforms, scroll-snap or scroll-driven animation; a scroller whose visible part is cut by a sibling overlay rather than an ancestor clip.
- **Package (A2):** JSON files (by suffix, or text starting with `{` or `[`) are parsed and every key is screened with `credential_key()` (any case or separator, camelCase too), 8+ character string values; the regex stays for other text and now names access/refresh tokens and secret/access keys case-insensitively. Known limit: credentials in YAML, TOML or `.env`-style text under keys the regex does not name; JSON with comments (not parseable) falls back to the regex only.
- **Contrast (A3):** CSS `opacity` on the text and its composed ancestors is composited (premultiplied groups); a `mix-blend-mode` on the chain is counted unknown. UNTESTED: `filter: opacity()`/`brightness()`, opacity set through the Web Animations API mid-animation, `mask`, and colour-mix or relative colours that compute to non-`rgb()` strings (counted unknown, not measured).
- **Designer process (B1-B4):** the complete wireframe gate now runs inside Step 5 after the task and findability tests; column counts are unitless; `feedback-log`, `emphasis-order`, `type-measure`, `colour-roles` and `grid-per-width` are manual IDs (the last four PASS or DEFERRED in the wireframe phase); every critic check names a rubric row and the final round needs a passing check per row. The contract checks that the records exist with evidence; whether the feedback log, emphasis check, type measure, colour matrix, grid and critic judgments are good is a reviewer judgment, not machine-checked. No real project was run through the new Step 5 order.

## R14 additions (Mi'mar, 18 October 2026, panel round 14)

All browser checks below ran in Chrome 155 headless only. Panel round 14 had one reviewer (GPT) and no cross-check; Mi'mar acted as the second Part B reviewer. Scope follows the threat model in `FANNAN-PLAN.md`.

- **Content and contrast (R14-GPT-01/02):** copy slots and headings count as rendered when their text ranges have area (`display: contents`, zero-size boxes); contrast walks open shadow roots and resolves backgrounds through slots and hosts. UNTESTED: text inside closed shadow roots (fails as an unscanned scope in the width screen, not in the contrast diagnostic); backgrounds painted by a slotted element's light-DOM ancestors that the slot's own composed chain does not include.
- **Width screen (R14-GPT-03/04):** closed shadow roots are counted at each width after a bounded settle (two animation frames or 250 ms); a viewport that cannot scroll vertically (html, or a propagated body, with `overflow: hidden`/`clip`) fails text and media below its bottom edge unless an inner scroller holds them. UNTESTED: a closed root created later than the settle; `position: sticky` content; horizontal reach on a hidden viewport beyond the existing overflow check.
- **Reduced motion (R14-GPT-05):** the snapshot covers the composed tree with composed-path keys, on the page and every state (executed fixture runs). UNTESTED: motion inside closed shadow roots or frames; motion that only starts after the 700 ms window.
- **Package (R14-GPT-06):** credential keys anywhere in `tokens.json` (every dict and list) are refused, and the content screen refuses JSON credential keys with real-looking values (8+ characters). Known limit: credentials under keys the list does not name, or values shorter than 8 characters.

