| R20-GPT-01 | A | P2 | `release/tools/width-screen.mjs:69–90` | **The clipping exemption both blocks ordinary inactive content and excuses accidental content loss.** It collects only descendants (`d !== e`) and accepts overflow only beyond the end edge. Executed DOM-model results: a collapsed accordion containing bare text still produces `clipped`; a carousel showing its middle slide produces `clipped` because the previous slide lies before the start edge; a fixed-height card whose entire body paragraph falls below its bottom produces **no failure entries**. The latter satisfies `out.every(...)` and skips the clipping failure despite having no disclosure interaction. These are ordinary patterns and mistakes. Related round-13 safeguards: **N01–N03**. | Scope inactive-content exemptions to identifiable components and their declared states, with evidence that hidden content becomes available in the appropriate state. Include the clipping element’s own text ranges. Permit accounted-for inactive carousel slides on either side while retaining start-edge failures for unrelated layout loss. Add regressions for bare-text collapsed panels, middle-slide LTR/RTL states and an accidentally hidden whole card paragraph. |
| R20-GPT-02 | A | P2 | `release/tools/visibility.mjs:223–224,259–260`; `release/references/design-quality.md:17` | **Any gradient pseudo-element on an overflow-clipping ancestor becomes an unconditional hiding exemption.** The rule is merely `fade: clipsOwn && /gradient/.test(s.backgroundImage)`. It does not require actual truncation, an edge-localized fade or an available expansion action. Executed `visibilityBand` DOM-model reproduction: an ordinary rounded card using `overflow:hidden`, covered by an inset `::after` gradient from 80% to 90% white, classifies its otherwise fully fitting text as `"hidden"` / `"under the fade of a truncated block"`. Pixels are never graded. This can conceal a stuck loading veil. It also contradicts the plan’s no-cut-copy rule and `design-quality.md:59`’s “no clipping, truncation.” | Treat gradients as possible covers by default. Grant a truncation exception only for an explicitly specified disclosure component, with bounded fade geometry and a verified route to the complete copy; apply the owner’s no-cut-copy requirement consistently. Add a negative regression for a full-card gradient loading veil on an overflow-hidden card, retaining the solid-veil regressions. |
| R20-GPT-03 | A | P2 | `release/tools/visibility.mjs:300–307,393–395,423–424` | **Photographic detail can satisfy the new AA resolver instead of the text.** `pixelContrast` calls every pixel differing from the rectangle’s modal color “ink,” then uses its 90th-percentile contrast. This includes the photograph itself. Executed pixel reproduction: pale text with actual foreground/local-background contrast **1.35:1**, plus a dark photographic stripe elsewhere in the line rectangle, returns **16.17:1**. Supplying those measurements for both renderings and a citing `wcag-review` makes `resolveContrastIncomplete` return `pass:true`. An ordinary bright photo with dark detail can therefore erase the axe incomplete that previously kept inadequate text contrast blocking. | Require evidence that separates foreground glyphs from the local background before using pixel measurements as AA proof. Do not resolve from unclassified image texture; retain INCOMPLETE where foreground/background contrast cannot be measured. Add a negative photograph fixture with low-contrast text and high-contrast background detail, alongside the existing adequately scrimmed positive fixture. |
